{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-61","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"61","title":"A joint resolution providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Bureau of Land Management relating to \"Miles City Field Office Record of Decision and Approved Resource Management Plan Amendment\".","introducedDate":"2025-07-10","latestActionDate":"2025-10-07","latestActionText":"Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 179.","policyArea":"Public Lands and Natural Resources","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Department of the Interior","Land use and conservation","Mining","Montana"],"sponsorBioguideId":"D000618","cosponsorBioguideIds":["S001232"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution nullifies the rule submitted by the Bureau of Land Management (BLM) relating to the Record of Decision and Approved Resource Management Plan (RMP) Amendment for the Miles City Field Office in Montana and issued on November 20, 2024. By way of background, such an amendment revises an RMP, which guides the management of lands administered by BLM. This Miles City Field Office RMP Amendment made no acres available for coal leasing and 1,745,040 acres unavailable for further consideration for coal leasing.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/61","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres61.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:25.819Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-61","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would overturn a Bureau of Land Management decision issued in November 2024 that closed approximately 1.7 million acres of federal land in Montana to future coal leasing. If enacted, the resolution would nullify that land-use plan amendment for the Miles City Field Office. The practical effect would be to restore the possibility of coal leasing on those acres, though it would not automatically open them to leasing.","industries":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill would reverse a decision that made 1.7 million acres unavailable for coal leasing, directly affecting coal extraction opportunities."},{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Coal leasing availability on federal lands directly affects mining operations and mineral extraction rights."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"dc8c15bb9e2a5055041b2ad7","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:40:55.750Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would cancel the rule submitted by the federal land agency relating to the Record of Decision and Approved Resource Management Plan Amendment for the Miles City Field Office in Montana and issued on November 20, 2024.","whoItTouches":"People who live near or work on federal land. People who live near a mine, and people who work in one.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what may be done with that land. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-sjres-61","bioguideId":"D000618","cycle":2026,"score":0.007302478428858675,"matches":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":39000,"donorShare":0.01125431362691282,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.005939776636426211},{"industry":"mining","donorAmount":10000,"donorShare":0.002885721442798159,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0013627017924324643}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","don